## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-account log delivery, least privilege, and lifecycle handling in a real SaaS pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account IAM roles and bucket policies
- TLS-only S3 access and private endpoints
- CMK encryption and CloudTrail validation
- Onboarding/offboarding automation and rollback

## Code Example
```json
// Central bucket policy sketch
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<central-id>:role/TenantLogPublisher"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::central-logs-bucket/tenant-prefix/*",
      "Condition": {
        "Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "true"}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
// Trust policy sketch for TenantLogPublisher (in central/account_root)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"Service": "delivery.logs.amazonaws.com"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:SourceAccount": "<central-id>"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test end-to-end log delivery across accounts?
- How would you handle rotation of the CMK and rotation of the tenant role?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross‑account IAM trust, least privilege, and S3 bucket policies for a logs sink. It also covers testing and revocation.

## Key Concepts
- Cross‑account IAM roles
- Trust policy with ExternalId
- Bucket policy for PutObject
- Minimal assuming policy for the partner
- Validation and revocation steps

## Code Example
```json
// Trust policy (LogSinkRole in Account A)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<AccountB>:role/PartnerLogSender"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"sts:ExternalId": "<external-id>"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
// Bucket policy in Account A (central-logs-bucket)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<AccountA>:role/LogSinkRole"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::central-logs-bucket/logs/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
// Partner policy (in Account B, PartnerLogSender role)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      ""Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      ""Resource": "arn:aws:iam::<AccountA>:role/LogSinkRole",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"sts:ExternalId": "<external-id>"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate or revoke the ExternalId and what would that affect?
- What monitoring would you add to detect failed assume-role or PutObject attempts?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design cross-region data residency, strict data isolation, and automated rollback in a multi-account AWS environment.

## Key Concepts

- AWS Organizations multi-account governance and service control
- Region-scoped S3 buckets and per-tenant KMS keys for data sovereignty
- Lake Formation permissions and principled data access
- PrivateLink for private connectivity to regional data-hubs
- Cross-region replication with tenant-level controls
- 60-second rollback using Step Functions + EventBridge
- Automated tests and drift/compliance checks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: basic per-tenant trust for cross-account role
const trustPolicy = {
  Version: "2012-10-17",
  Statement: [{
    Effect: "Allow",
    Principal: { AWS: "arn:aws:iam::TENANT_ACCOUNT:role/TenantAppRole" },
    Action: "sts:AssumeRole"
  }]
};

// Minimal Step Functions skeleton for rollback workflow (pseudo)
const sfDefinition = {
  Comment: "Tenant data residency rollback",
  StartAt: "Validate",
  States: {
    Validate: { Type: "Pass", Next: "Rollback" },
    Rollback: { Type: "Fail", Cause: "Trigger rollback" }
  }
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate data residency compliance across new tenants during signup?
- What monitoring and alerting would you add to detect cross-region replication drift and ensure timely remediation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical data isolation, policy enforcement, and automated remediation in a multi-tenant AWS setup.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-prefix isolation in S3
- IAM condition keys (aws:SourceAccount, aws:SourceArn)
- Per-tenant KMS keys and grants
- Service Controls Policies to prevent cross-tenant writes
- Config-based drift detection and EventBridge-backed remediation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example bucket resource policy (conceptual)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::TENANT_ACCOUNT:role/TenantIngress"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/tenant-prefix-*/**",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "s3:prefix": "tenant-prefix-*"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-tenant data leakage exit paths?
- How would you roll back a misconfigured bucket policy without downtime?
